Focus Feed Next - exhaust every bookmark

The provider's generic timeline guard is useful for ordinary reads, but the
Bookmarks endpoint has its own terminal cursor. Applying the generic
max-count guard makes a complete account look truncated at the provider's
configured safety value.

This patch is applied by tools/twitter-cli/apply_patch.py to the pinned
twitter-cli installation. It changes only the Bookmarks operation:

--- a/twitter_cli/client.py
+++ b/twitter_cli/client.py
@@
-        return self._fetch_timeline("Bookmarks", count, get_instructions)
+        return self._fetch_timeline(
+            "Bookmarks",
+            count,
+            get_instructions,
+            respect_max_count=False,
+        )
@@
-    def _fetch_timeline(..., return_cursor=False):
+    def _fetch_timeline(..., return_cursor=False, respect_max_count=True):
@@
-    # Enforce max count cap
-    count = min(count, self._max_count)
+    if respect_max_count:
+        count = min(count, self._max_count)

The app passes u32::MAX for bookmarks. The provider continues its internal
cursor until the endpoint returns no next cursor; ordinary feeds retain the
provider's normal safety cap. The application still paints the first screen
from its local cache immediately and performs the exhaustive bookmark sync in
the background.
